A||||5||BOILERMAKER||Retrofit from R12 to R134||||1||
Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||03-14-2000||08:15 AM||||First of all, I am making no money from this guy or his company by telling everyone this!<P>I did a retrofit from R12 to R134 last summer on my black GT (86) and I had a chance to test it again this past couple of weeks when it was warm.  It still works great and I am very very pleased.  I feel as if I do not have to recharge the system this summer (now keep in mind I have only had this retrofit for around a year now!)<P>His email is rmays6900@aol.com and he is extremely helpful in explaining the whole process.  He reps a company and I bought a lot of flush (1 gal) and esther oil (can't remember exact qty) from him, so I have enough to do the gold GT this spring.<P>I replace my dryer and a couple of O-rings (the ones that were exposed when I got the car) and the system doesn't leak (at least my system doesn't leak).<P>If you are interested in doing this, then it doesn't hurt to drop him a line.  If you decide not to use his product, no skin off my back.  I figured I would offer his services to anyone who needs them because he was so helpful with me.||||reg||1
Z||000001||GWMORGAN||03-14-2000||09:54 AM||||What's the cost for the retro-fit?||||reg||1
Z||000002||BOILERMAKER||03-14-2000||10:00 AM||||Email him for all of the specific's.  I got the flush, oil, orifice tube, instructions, adapter fittings, and gun to send the flush through the system and I believe it all came up to around $100 or $90, but it's been around a year and I would have to check to make sure.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||03-14-2000||12:49 PM||||Try Interdynamics' kit.  About $35 at most auto parts stores.  Includes everything.  You replace no components (but there is nothing wrong with doing that if you want to).  They have a web site also that talks about the conversion and answers a lot of common questions and mentions  many common beliefs and theories about the right way to do it.  The only problem I had was locating and fixing an exsisting leak that was not related to the conversion.  Been pretty happy with the results.  A guage should be used to ensure you refill with the correct amount.||||reg||7
Z||000004||BOILERMAKER||03-14-2000||02:11 PM||||I guess I should have added the following.<P>If you can find it elsewhere for less money, then go ahead and do it if you feel like it is reliable (who's really to say in these situations unless someone has tried it).<P>I am just offering an alternative (because I have done it) and, like I said before, if it's not the way you want to go, then that's fine.  I will try to help with any more information that I have.||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||03-14-2000||02:55 PM||||Boilermaker,<P>Thanks for starting this topic.  I had wanted to do this for some time now, but assumed it was much harder than it sounds.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
